            <ArticleTitle>The effect of coronary angioplasty on superoxide anion generation and lipid peroxidation. Propriety of pharmacological intervention.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>163-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In patients with unstable angina pectoris, subjected (n = 20) or not subjected (n = 12) to percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty (PTCA), baseline superoxide anion (O.2-) generation by neutrophils in the coronary sinus blood was significantly higher than that found in the basilic vein blood of control healthy subjects (n = 12). During reperfusion following effective PTCA, neutrophil counts in the coronary sinus blood tended to decrease, an effect accompanied by a significant decrease in the neutrophil O.2- generation and enhancement of blood plasma lipid peroxidation as reflected by increased malonyldialdehyde concentrations.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
